\begin{document}
\title{PDEs and the Green’s Function in Physics}
\author{Hariharan Senthilkumar}
\date{July 14, 2025}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
    This paper covers PDEs and discusses physical applications such as transport, waves, vibration, and diffusion. We will derive several key theorems about PDEs, and we will spend a lot of time discussing the heat and wave equations. We will also talk about boundary problems and how they tie the Green's function and PDEs together. All information, images, ideas, proofs, structure, and so on come directly from the sources cited below, especially Strauss. The whole paper is based completely on these textbooks, with original commentary and analysis provided by the author.

\end{abstract}
\section{Notation}
Let us go over some commonly used notation in this paper. We will cover more of this later on, but it is still important to define it up top for consistency. This following is tge notation we will use for three dimensions, but similar notation will be used for two dimensions and one dimension: $$\text{grad} f=\nabla f=\text{vector} (f_x,f_y,f_z)$$ $$\text{div }\mathbf{F}=\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F}=\frac{\partial F_1}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial F_2}{\partial y}+\frac{\partial F_3}{\partial z},$$ $$\Delta u=\text{div grad }u=\nabla\cdot\nabla u=u_{xx}+u_{yy}+u_{zz},$$ $$|\nabla u|^2=|\text{grad }u|^2=u_{x}^2+u_y^2+u_z^2,$$ where $\mathbf{F}=(F_1,F_2,F_3)$ is a vector field. In this paper, we rely completely on Strauss's notation and ideas, so we write the Laplacian $\nabla\cdot\nabla$ as $\Delta,$ not $\nabla^2$. Also, we write the derivatives by subscripts, so $$\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}=u_x, \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}=u_y,...$$

\section{PDEs: General}
\cite{strauss2007partial} \cite{marsden2003vector}
In ordinary differential equations (ODEs), the function depends on a single variable, but in PDEs we focus on multiple independent variables $x,y,z,...$. PDEs also have a dependent variable (which is the unknown function of the aforementioned independent variables) that we write as $u(x,y,z,...)$.
